WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.210
Ron: This group in the middle that we're concerned
with up to, say, $60,000, $65,000 a year.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.240
Robert: Household income, yes.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.760
Ron: They are the ones we need to help.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:17.300
And they don't get nearly as much help as
this bottom group does.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:27.810
Robert: Ron Haskins, long-time expert on issues
concerning struggling Americans at the Brookings

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.220
Institution.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.220
Thanks for being here.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.220
Ron: Glad to be here.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:36.110
Robert: So, you worked with scholars from
both the left and the right to produce a report,

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.160
Work, Skills, Community.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.570
Restoring opportunity for the working class.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:45.870
Tell me about the facts concerning this group
that you thought needed attention.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.300
Ron: There are a whole set facts.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:54.200
The first chapter in the volume, after introduction
and a bunch of preliminary material, is about

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:58.219
the facts on which we built the rest of the
arguments.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:01.730
We describe roughly how many people there
are.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.149
There are the people between who are...have
a high school degree but they do not have

00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:07.149
a four-year degree.

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:10.329
They live, of course, all over the United
States.

00:01:10.329 --> 00:01:13.549
We show a map where they're concentrated.

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:18.159
A number of them are concentrated in areas
that won't surprise you, like the Appalachian

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:23.149
area, Ohio, places that have had high unemployment
in the past.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:28.280
But these are people who are...they're making
it but barely.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.609
And many of them are not going up.

00:01:31.609 --> 00:01:35.079
They're struggling to keep their heads above
water.

00:01:35.079 --> 00:01:41.219
And in describing these people, having met
a number of them and visited...we both visited

00:01:41.219 --> 00:01:45.279
a number of these groups, I would emphasize
struggling and..

00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:46.490
They have not given up.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:47.490
They're trying.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.169
They're trying to help their kids.

00:01:50.169 --> 00:01:55.020
But I do think a number of them are pretty
discouraged and they did not expect what has

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.029
happened to them.

00:01:57.029 --> 00:02:01.459
And that shows up in the facts about what
their financial condition is.

00:02:01.459 --> 00:02:11.420
And especially, we have a section on debt
and on wealth, which affects...you know, net,

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.930
amount that their worth.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:17.879
And for almost all families, a lot of people
don't know this, for almost all families,

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:21.909
except really wealthy families, it's how much
they have in their house.

00:02:21.909 --> 00:02:27.989
And so many of these families lost their home
during the recession and they haven't recovered

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:28.989
completely.

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:33.400
So, they have zero or close to zero wealth.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:34.900
And that's gonna affect their retirement.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.720
And they don't have enough money to make it
up.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.000
So, there are lots of issues here that we
try to spell out in that facts chapter.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:46.350
So, anybody who reads the report can start
off with, you know, with the space of set

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.350
of facts.

00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:51.190
One other thing that I would mention that
I think is very important, I think there's

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:57.109
a growing awareness of this in the country
but drugs are a big problem.

00:02:57.109 --> 00:03:03.280
And we have a great team of researchers from
Princeton, who described this problem in great

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.310
detail, others have as well.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:09.110
And we've had a number of books in the last
three or four years.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.859
You have a great expert here at AEI, Sally
Satel.

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:19.319
And this group has not only expanded but they've
gone deeper and deeper in the hole.

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:22.749
And many of them, it does appear to be hopeless
and, in fact, they die.

00:03:22.749 --> 00:03:28.319
So, we have what...a Nobel Prize winner called
"Deaths of Despair."

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:34.519
And we give data on that problem, it has increased
greatly, and it's concentrated in the same

00:03:34.519 --> 00:03:38.420
areas as these working-class families.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:43.469
So, that's another big problem, and we really
don't have any good solutions for that.

00:03:43.469 --> 00:03:49.440
Robert: So, stagnating wages, declining wealth,
exposure to drug addiction, and serious health

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.620
issues, a feeling of hopelessness.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.249
Politics in Washington, has it responded to
their needs?

00:03:56.249 --> 00:04:00.859
Ron: If it has, it has not been a great response.

00:04:00.859 --> 00:04:04.219
We just passed an opioid bill, as you know.

00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:05.719
Maybe that'll have some effects.

00:04:05.719 --> 00:04:11.550
In the past, this kind of legislation has
not had much of an impact.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.200
A lot of people think if Washington responds
and does the right thing it'll make a big

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.220
difference, that usually is not the case.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.530
You could have marginal effects.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:26.550
But over a period of years, if you have a
number of good policies, like what we propose,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.310
then it could make a difference.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.699
So, that's what we're hoping.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:36.699
We're hoping and a lot of simultaneous changes
in policy that are applied evenly and wisely

00:04:36.699 --> 00:04:39.650
over a period of years would have an impact
on this problem.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:40.650
So, reduce...

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:41.650
Robert: At the local level and at a federal
level?

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:45.110
Ron: Yes, at the local and state...well, I'm
thinking local and state level.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.110
So, we apply it.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.080
We do something about opioids.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.860
We do something about wages.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.240
We do something about employment.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.870
We do something about community.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.610
I think all these things have a potential
to make a difference.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:04.139
Robert: So, one more preliminary question
that...we went down to Kentucky to see something

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:06.699
and to get a sense of a successful program.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:07.699
What did we see?

00:05:07.699 --> 00:05:14.930
Ron: The most impressive thing to me was...and
I love this in part of it because it was undertaken

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:20.830
by the private sector, primarily automobile
companies, but it's expanded now.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:29.389
And Toyota created a program in which they
focused on kids who are high-school graduates

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:31.160
and were pretty smart.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:36.610
It wasn't, you know, kids who are doing the
worst from high school, but kids who are,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:41.819
say, roughly in the middle, and who do not
have plans to go to a four-year college.

00:05:41.819 --> 00:05:43.819
And this, of course, is right...

00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:45.091
Robert: Right, at the heart of our...

00:05:45.091 --> 00:05:46.091
Right.

00:05:46.091 --> 00:05:47.759
Ron: ...in our sweet spot, the group that
we're concerned about.

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:54.210
And let me say that I have filed this research
for a number of years and I think, as a nation,

00:05:54.210 --> 00:06:00.590
we've made a mistake by overemphasizing the
importance of four-year education.

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:04.810
Now, I think you have to be real cautious
here because I love the idea of four-year

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:05.810
education.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.650
I want my kids to get a four-year education.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.750
I was intent myself on getting a four-year
education.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:15.800
But we have millions of kids who...they're
not gonna do well on a four-year institution

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.599
because they're not prepared for it.

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:21.949
And we have encouraged many of them to go
to college and they went to college.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:22.949
They didn't finish.

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:24.379
They wound up with debt.

00:06:24.379 --> 00:06:26.889
So, now, they have the worst of both worlds.

00:06:26.889 --> 00:06:29.680
They don't have better jobs and they have
debt from college.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.590
So, we need a strategy that helps people get
in the middle there and get jobs that pay

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:38.960
$10,000 or $20,000 more than the typical,
you know...

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.470
Robert: Entry-level jobs?

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:42.870
Ron: ...$10-an-hour job.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:47.220
And we actually have pretty good studies that
show that we can do that.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.750
And this program is called Kentucky FAME.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:53.419
And they now have...it started out with just
a couple of employers.

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:58.490
It was designed primarily by employers, which,
by the way, years of research have shown,

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:03.439
the most successful training programs are
the ones that employers start and employers

00:07:03.439 --> 00:07:04.560
run.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.780
And the Federal Government needs to figure
out how to subsidize them and not design a

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:10.520
program and say, "Here's what you have to
do."

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:14.060
The employers are the ones that know what
they need, they know the skills they need

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.530
to emphasize, and that's what we saw in this
program.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:22.720
We saw a beautiful place where they train
these kids and give them the skills in electronics,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:27.470
in welding, the kind of things that, you know...where
there are a lot of jobs available.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:33.389
And they pay, you know, $15, $20, $25 an hour,
and they have a future.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:37.410
So, that was very encouraging and they are
doing well.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.621
They've even expanded since we were there.

00:07:38.621 --> 00:07:41.259
We were there a little over a year ago.

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:45.960
So, I think there are now 300 employers that
are involved in that Kentucky FAME program.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:46.960
So...

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:50.379
Robert: That's the kind of thing that we talk
about and recommend and think needs to be

00:07:50.379 --> 00:07:52.560
incentivized by the Federal Government.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:59.110
So, turning a little bit to what Washington
can or cannot do, you have been a long-time

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:03.919
supporter of the...something called the Earned
Income Tax Credit, which tops up wages for

00:08:03.919 --> 00:08:07.439
families with children in the household, often
single mothers.

00:08:07.439 --> 00:08:12.169
I think, Ron, you're the one who first taught
me about the ways in which work requirements

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:17.039
blended with work supports can really bring
families out of poverty and has really brought

00:08:17.039 --> 00:08:18.569
families out of poverty.

00:08:18.569 --> 00:08:22.810
We make a recommendation about another group
that's been left out of that work support

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:23.810
system.

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:24.810
Tell us about that.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:25.810
Ron: Right.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:31.000
Well, I think the broader point here is that
we have a lot of things that support single

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:32.170
mothers.

00:08:32.170 --> 00:08:35.621
And they would support single fathers if they
were custodial single fathers, but most of

00:08:35.621 --> 00:08:36.621
them are not.

00:08:36.621 --> 00:08:40.930
So, we support...We have a lot of programs
and this Earned Income Tax Credit is one.

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:43.050
And it can be $6,000, $7,000 a year.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:45.720
So this is very serious benefit.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.050
We spend about $70 billion on a program.

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.740
So, this is serious policy here.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:52.650
We don't have anything cool enough for men.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:09:00.910
We have a dinky little $400, $500 a year maximum
benefit for them if they go to work and earn

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:02.090
a wage.

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:09.050
So, we would like to increase that and we
propose substantially increasing that for...it

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:13.060
would be primarily single fathers who would
benefit from it.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:20.660
But these are individuals who go to work and
have earnings and are non-custodial.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:21.660
They don't have kids.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:28.440
And many of them have kids that they don't
live with and they pay child support.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.240
We actually have a very good study in New
York City.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.310
We have another one going on in Atlanta.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:39.270
The study from New York City, this was perfectly
timed with our report by coincidence.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:45.620
It showed that there was actually an increase
in the number of workers...of men, primarily

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:47.620
men who go to work.

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.540
They have increase in earnings.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.630
And they have an increase in their payment
of child support.

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:57.300
So, all three of those outcomes are very nice,
it's a good thing that happened.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:02.090
It's a very good company named Manpower Demonstration
Research Corporation that demonstrated those

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:03.110
effects.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.640
And now, they have another one going on in
Atlanta.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.630
We'll see if we have the same effects there.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:08.830
These are not huge effects.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:13.360
We wish they were bigger but they're still
impressive and they're significant.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.650
They're statistically significant.

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:22.510
Robert: This is one of those right and left
combination things where we try to get people

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:23.850
to agree.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:29.360
One thing that I think was important to me
and I think was important to you and I wanted

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:35.090
you to sort of describe why it was important,
was that it's deficit neutral or spending

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:36.090
neutral.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.660
Tell us about that.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:44.580
Ron: The left seems to be less intense about
this but the members of our group on the left

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.620
were...they seem to be right where we were.

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:49.840
You know, the deficit is too big.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:51.100
We gotta do something about the deficit.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:54.790
And God knows, in the long run, what the consequence
is gonna be.

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:58.500
We're already, you know, in the red zone.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.230
We're gonna be hurt in the long run by this.

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:04.440
Robert: The red zone and the extent with that,
the percentage of debt to GDP or the size

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:09.140
of our deficit is so high that we've lost
opportunities to address it sooner.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:10.180
And now, it's getting worse.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.960
Ron: Well, yes, and there will be consequences.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:20.270
That's what I mean by the red zone, especially
impacts on economic growth, a number of studies

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:21.700
show that.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:25.500
So, we're in danger territory and we gotta
do something about it.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:31.240
And the worst thing is that Washington seems...they're
acting like they're not aware of it.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.750
They have to be aware of it but they're not
doing anything.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:35.360
There's no serious action.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.980
There are no serious proposals on the table.

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.670
And, in fact, many of their proposals that
are more seriously being discussed now would

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:44.400
increase it even more.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:49.160
Trump has a proposal, apparently, a lot of
support among Republicans, to increase it

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:54.990
by another trillion dollars by a middle...he
calls it a middle-class tax cut.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.950
So, this is really a very serious problem.

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.340
And we wanted to, at least, be neutral.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:03.570
We wanted not to increase the debt problem.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.490
Robert: So, we found revenues from where?

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.920
Ron: So, we found revenue offsets.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:14.670
And we made a number of changes in tax policy
and in spending policy to offset...I'm not

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:19.140
sure you need a CBO, Congressional Budget
Officer, estimate to really let you know if

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:20.320
you achieved that.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.750
But, at least, it's a signal that we were
concerned about this problem and we did something

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:25.750
about it.

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:28.480
Robert: So, you've been in Washington a long
time, Ron.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:34.590
And, you know, the cliché is that nothing
ever gets done and bipartisanship never really

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.750
happens or, at least, that's the cliché now.

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:37.860
Is that true?

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:38.860
Ron: No.

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:39.860
Robert: Tell us...

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:40.860
Ron: No, it's not true.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:44.810
I think last year we did a lot of really interesting
things.

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:50.260
We increased childcare support for working
families.

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:54.940
That's a very good thing because we wanna
help workers.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:13:02.880
We changed the Child Tax Credit, which has
the same effect and helps low-income families.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:08.720
We passed a number of bills having to do with
education and with related issues.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:10.510
So, there have been...

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:11.990
And all of these are bipartisan.

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:12.990
We also did...

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:18.750
I don't wanna bore anybody about this but
I've been following child protection issues,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.630
abuse and neglect for many years.

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:26.300
And we've tried to have for many, many years
now, trying to create a program that would

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:33.470
help states figure out ways to keep the families
in their homes from the very beginning.

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:38.260
Now, this is a risky business because most
of them have been involved in abuse or neglect.

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:43.640
So, you wanna work with the families, keep
the kids safe and work with the families to

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:47.560
reduce the problems that the families are
having, so we don't have to remove the kids

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:49.310
from their home.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:55.090
Our history shows that if we remove kids from
the home, the outcomes are not good.

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:56.370
This is a very...

00:13:56.370 --> 00:14:02.300
Sometimes, you have to do it because the kids
are...you know, they're physically threatened.

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:06.750
But once you do that, there are long-term
consequences and it's hard to get the kids

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:07.940
in a stable setting.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:09.540
So, this new program called Family...

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:12.660
Robert: Which is passed by Congress and signed
by the President.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:17.370
Ron: On a bipartisan basis, signed by the
President called Family First, would provide

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:24.070
more money to the states to design these programs,
if they're based on evidence, which is another,

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:25.450
I think, very strong thing.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:30.750
And, by the way, as a footnote to my response
to you, a lot of the programs that have gotten

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:34.710
support now are evidence-based programs or
try to promote evidence-based programs.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:39.310
So, there have been a number of programs in
the last couple of years that are both based

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:44.990
on evidence, where reasonably they might be
effective and they enjoyed bipartisan support.

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:50.290
Robert: So, in your judgment, a bipartisan
group puts the other rapport with a whole

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:57.030
host of ideas, notwithstanding what we've
seen in CNBC or Fox News, your view would

00:14:57.030 --> 00:14:58.800
be based on, what?

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:01.870
Thirty-five years of experience here at Washington?

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:07.290
Would be there's a good chance the way things
happen in this world that good ideas do lead

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:11.210
to better legislation and do lead to positive
outcomes in the country.

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:13.020
Ron: Yes, that is correct.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.660
And I am far from giving up on this idea.

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:23.530
The one caveat is it often takes a number
of years to really penetrate, you know, the

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:24.530
Congress.

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:29.570
So, if we have a new Congress, at least a
new House of Representatives, which seems

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:34.850
slightly less than 50-50 now, just a guess.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:39.390
Then, the Congress, especially the House will
be trying to do new things.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:43.930
And some of them will involve spending, social
policy would be very much on their agenda.

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:48.820
So, they may be looking around for stuff like
what we did and we could go there.

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:51.830
And we might able to present the same ideas
in the Senate.

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:56.660
Let's say that the Republicans do keep the
Senate and they might be attracted to the

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:57.660
ideas, too.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:02.490
We had very strong Republican members of this
group as well as members on the left.

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:06.000
So, our proposals can appeal to both sides.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.000
For some...

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:08.690
Robert: So, just last sort of question.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:14.040
You spend most of your career focusing on
people at the very bottom, and in advocating,

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:17.770
and researching, and describing solutions
that can help them.

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:24.470
The fact is we've had some success at reducing
poverty, reducing child poverty in the United

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:25.470
States.

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:27.890
People on the left and right agree with that
and partly because we spent a lot of money

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:28.890
on it.

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:31.840
Ron: You have to admit though, they don't
seem to have noticed it very much.

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:35.950
Robert: Yes, I know, but the numbers are stronger
and better, in terms of getting people up

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:41.550
to that first ladder, but this other group
was kinda unattended, too.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:47.440
And so, do you think you'll have a chance
for similar success now that Ron Haskins has

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:48.850
turned his attention to...

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:50.610
Ron: I don't think I'll have much of an impact.

00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:51.610
Robert: I don't agree.

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:57.270
Ron: But this is a very, very good point that
we've had good success at the bottom.

00:16:57.270 --> 00:17:01.050
And that's the most basic thing and it's good
that we did that.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:06.740
And we've reduced poverty for sure and our
programs have done...and we have shown that

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:11.269
low-income workers are way better off than
they were in the past.

00:17:11.269 --> 00:17:14.730
I think that's a success too, that the message
goes forth.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:18.179
You gotta work, you know, and that's a good
thing because you're not gonna move up further

00:17:18.179 --> 00:17:19.610
unless you do work.

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:25.419
But this group in the middle that we're concerned
with that, you know, from up to, say, $60,000,

00:17:25.419 --> 00:17:26.789
$65,000 a year...

00:17:26.789 --> 00:17:28.669
Robert: Household income, yes.

00:17:28.669 --> 00:17:31.360
Ron: They are the ones we need to help.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:35.110
And they don't get nearly as much help as
this bottom group does.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:38.280
So, that's where our proposals are aimed.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:44.179
And I hope we can make some progress and I'm
not at all pessimistic that we could make

00:17:44.179 --> 00:17:45.179
progress.

00:17:45.179 --> 00:17:47.220
And that if we did, this group would be helped.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.120
Robert: Ron Haskins, thanks for being here.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:50.379
Ron: Glad to be here.

00:17:50.379 --> 00:17:51.379
Thank you very much.

00:17:51.379 --> 00:17:53.409
Robert: Hi, everyone.

00:17:53.409 --> 00:17:56.080
That's the end of our discussion with Ron
Haskins.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:57.080
Thanks for watching.

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:02.220
And as always, let us know what other topics
you'd like AEI Scholars to cover on "Viewpoint."

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:08.039
And to learn more about the working class
in America, check the links in the description

00:18:08.039 --> 00:18:08.389
below.

